# Local additions to the login context map - contexts/users/<selinux-user>.
#
# Like fcontexts.local and booleans.local, this lives outside the module store
# (it is a refpolicy data file installed by sec-policy/selinux-base-policy, not
# a rule), so the repo records it with the command that reapplies it.
#
#   contexts/users/staff_u
#
#     staff_r:staff_t:s0               staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023   staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#
#   Why: every other key in that file is a *login program* - local_login_t,
#   remote_login_t, sshd_t, crond_t, xdm_t, and the su/sudo wrappers. The OpenRC
#   user session is started by none of them: PAM resolves the xdm login to
#   staff_r:staff_t, and openrc-user then opens a SECOND PAM session from that
#   same context. With no key for staff_r:staff_t the lookup fell through to
#   staff_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0, and the exec of the session shell was refused
#   - not for want of an allow (`allow staff_t sysadm_t:process transition`
#   exists) but by the MCS constraint, because the source carried
#   s0-s0:c0.c1023 and the resolved target carried only s0.
#
#   Consequence: the user runlevel never ran at all. dsh, llama, adb, emulator
#   and protonmail all failed on every enforcing boot, and `failed/` stayed
#   empty because OpenRC never reached the point of failing anything.
#
#   Reapply after sec-policy/selinux-base-policy is reinstalled, which rewrites
#   this file:
#
#     printf '%s\n' \
#       'staff_r:staff_t:s0\t\t\tstaff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023' \
#       'staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023\tstaff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023' \
#       >> /etc/selinux/mcs/contexts/users/staff_u
#
#   Note there is no users/sysadm_u file at all, so a session for `stem`
#   (seusers: sysadm_u) has the same gap - unverified, because the desktop login
#   is `branch`.
staff_r:staff_t:s0               staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023   staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023

#   contexts/users/sysadm_u   (NEW FILE - it did not exist at all)
#
#   Why: seusers maps `stem` to sysadm_u, and with no file for it every lookup
#   fell through to default_contexts, whose targets carry a bare s0 while the
#   session carries s0-s0:c0.c1023. Whatever came back was then refused by the
#   MCS constraint (l1 eq l2) - the staff_r:staff_t trap again, one SELinux user
#   over. Latent until tested in the right identity, because the desktop login is
#   `branch`.
#
#   Reapply after sec-policy/selinux-base-policy is reinstalled:
#
#     cat > /etc/selinux/mcs/contexts/users/sysadm_u <<'SYSADM'
#     system_r:init_t:s0		sysadm_r:sysadm_systemd_t:s0
#     system_r:local_login_t:s0	sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     system_r:remote_login_t:s0	sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     system_r:sshd_t:s0		sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     system_r:crond_t:s0		sysadm_r:cronjob_t:s0
#     system_r:xdm_t:s0		sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     sysadm_r:sysadm_su_t:s0		sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     sysadm_r:sysadm_sudo_t:s0	sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0		sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023	sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     staff_r:staff_t:s0			sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023	sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     SYSADM
#
#   contexts/users/staff_u and contexts/users/root  -  ADDITIONAL KEYS
#
#   Why: the same class for a SECOND session opened from a context that is not a
#   login program. A sudo/root session runs as sysadm_r:sysadm_t, and neither file
#   had a key for it, so the lookup fell to failsafe_context and the exec was
#   refused on the range. Identity keys keep a session in the context it already
#   has, with the range intact:
#
#     staff_u:  sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0              -> sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#               sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023  -> sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#     root:     staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023    -> sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#               sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023  -> sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#
#   Verification - getconlist takes the SELINUX user, not the login name, and
#   wants a FULL context as the fromcon (a bare role:type:range is rejected as
#   invalid, which is what made a first round of probes look like a libselinux
#   bug when the caller was simply wrong):
#
#     getconlist sysadm_u system_u:system_r:xdm_t:s0
#       -> sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0        (pam applies the seusers level)
#     getconlist sysadm_u sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#       -> sysadm_u:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023    identity, full range
#     getconlist root root:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#       -> root:sysadm_r:sysadm_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023        identity, full range
#     getconlist staff_u staff_u:staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#       -> staff_u:staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023       the r26 key, still good
#
#   Confirmed live, not just on paper: the running branch session is
#   staff_u:staff_r:staff_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023, and a second PAM session opened from
#   it (pamsess harness, svc=openrc-user) execs /bin/bash successfully.
